                               GMime Examples
                   by Jeffrey Stedfast <fejj@gnome.org>


Example: basic-example.c
------------------------

This example code illustrates a good number of the common tasks an
application developer using GMime is likely to use, including parsing
messages, verifying pgp signatures, adding/removing MIME parts and
writing messages back out again.


Example: imap-example.c
-----------------------

Although the name implies that this example source contains t0p
s3kret imap hackery action, in reality, it doesn't. The focus of this
example is more how to split out MIME parts into separate files and
then to restore them back into the original MIME structure using a
technique similar to (but not exactly) IMAP's BODYSTRUCTURE and
single-part FETCHing.

I think you'll find this example is worth looking at if you plan on
using GMime with your IMAP library.


Example: uuencode.c
-------------------

This program illustrates how to use the low-level MIME encoding
routines (specifically the uuencode and base64 encoding
routines). You'll likely find that this program outperforms GNU's
implementation of uuencode.


Example: uudecode.c
-------------------

This program illustrates how to use the low-level MIME decoding
routines (specifically the uudecode and base64 decoding
routines). You'll likely find that this program outperforms GNU's
implementation of uudecode.


Example: mime-analyzer.c
------------------------

A C port of MimeKit's MimeAnalyzer sample. It uses GMimeReader's
compliance logger to scan messages without constructing a MIME object
tree, and reports compiler-style diagnostics with source lines, carets,
optional explanations and per-file summaries.

Build the mime-analyzer target with Meson (examples are enabled by
default on non-Windows hosts), then run:

  mime-analyzer [options] file.eml [file.eml ...]
  mime-analyzer --remarks message.eml
  mime-analyzer --context storage --severity major message.eml
  mime-analyzer --category security --werror message.eml
  mime-analyzer --max-issues 100 --max-per-violation 5 message.eml

Each input is a single message, not an mbox. Options:

  -c, --context transport|storage   Severity context (default: transport).
  -s, --severity minor|major|critical
                                   Minimum severity (default: minor).
      --category LIST              Comma-separated interoperability
                                   (or interop), dataloss, security.
  -r, --remarks                    Explain issues and their categories.
  -W, --werror                     Treat every reported issue as an error.
      --max-issues N               Collector limit (default: 1000).
      --max-per-violation N        Parser reporting limit per violation
                                   (default: 0, unlimited).
      --no-caret                   Do not quote source lines.
      --no-color                   Disable terminal colors.
  -q, --quiet                      Print only summaries.
  -h, --help                       Show help and exit.

Critical issues are errors, major issues warnings, and minor issues
notes. Filters apply before counting and --werror promotion. A filtered
report with no findings says "no matching compliance issues" rather
than claiming the message is clean. Reporting limits are independent
of these filters: reaching either limit produces a truncation note,
even when all collected findings are filtered out. The parser's
TooManyComplianceIssues signal is not counted as a message defect.

Source positions refer to raw input bytes, not decoded text. Tabs use
eight-column stops; control and non-ASCII bytes appear as \xNN. Carets
account for these expansions, and long lines are elided around the
caret to fit the terminal (100 source columns when redirected). Where
possible, approximate positions are refined to the offending byte,
including bytes in folded headers. Unknown columns have no caret.
Colors are automatically disabled for redirected output or when the
NO_COLOR environment variable is set.

Exit status is 0 if no reported issue is an error, 1 for error-level
issues or input/output failures (including an empty input that cannot
be parsed), and 2 for invalid command lines. Multiple files are scanned
independently, and processing continues after an input failure.


Don't forget to take a look at any test programs that came bundled
with this distribution (they will be in tests/), as they will also
provide you with samples that you may find useful.

Questions and/or Comments on these examples should be sent to
<fejj@gnome.org>
